# A7 6.03  Pythagorean Theorem — MA.8.GR.1.1 (MN 6.3). The square corner first: the side across from it is c, the
# longest side. The hypotenuse: square the legs, add, take the root. A leg: subtract the squares — and check that it
# is shorter than the hypotenuse. An irrational answer is exact (√n) and, when the question asks, to the nearest
# tenth. Every radicand is a whole number up to 225 (GR.1.1, Clarification 3) with no square factor, so √n is
# already in simplest form (the unit's standing decision; the audit's §6). Built from the benchmark and the B1G-M
# first (the unit outline), then read against Math Nation 6.3 (A7 Unit 6 Audit).
